Okay.

00:04:29.005 --> 00:04:29.725
So,

00:04:30.365 --> 00:04:32.685
what you're looking at is a

00:04:33.165 --> 00:04:34.205
diagram

00:04:34.605 --> 00:04:35.405
of

00:04:35.885 --> 00:04:37.165
the issues

00:04:37.165 --> 00:04:39.405
that the town is having

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:42.045
with the Bunker Hill

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:44.030
triple culvert.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.510
So, is a triple culvert.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:49.470
It has been steadily deteriorating

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.550
for the last fifteen years.

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:55.710
This is something that should have been replaced a long time ago.

00:04:57.205 --> 00:05:00.965
But it has not been because the town has not wanted to fund it.

00:05:03.765 --> 00:05:04.805
We are

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:09.605
basically going for a temporary repair

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:12.040
to get us through until such point as

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.920
a full bridge replacement

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:15.960
can be done,

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:20.200
which should be starting sometime within the next eighteen months.

00:05:21.495 --> 00:05:22.295
The,

00:05:22.935 --> 00:05:25.975
as you are all probably aware,

00:05:26.055 --> 00:05:28.455
so far this summer, we've had

00:05:29.255 --> 00:05:32.215
three fairly significant flood events.

00:05:33.175 --> 00:05:36.050
One of them was a ten year frequency

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:37.250
storm event.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:44.610
One was a two year frequency storm event and the last one was a two hundred year frequency storm event.

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:47.250
And all three of those cases,

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:48.130
the water,

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:50.370
you know, twice

00:05:51.195 --> 00:05:53.275
the water topped

00:05:53.595 --> 00:05:55.915
the culvert itself

00:05:55.915 --> 00:05:58.075
and ran over the culvert.

00:05:58.635 --> 00:05:59.915
As many of you know,

00:06:00.395 --> 00:06:01.755
to the north,

00:06:01.995 --> 00:06:06.795
along the road, there's a low point in the road that that the water goes over the road first.

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:13.330
And then when the water gets about two feet deeper than that, the water actually goes over the culvert.

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:14.610
And

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:20.865
so, the big problem we have is culvert number three, which is the northernmost

00:06:20.865 --> 00:06:21.745
culvert

00:06:21.985 --> 00:06:28.785
has a bunch of fairly large holes in the bottom of the culvert. It's basically completely rusted out on the bottom,

00:06:29.105 --> 00:06:30.465
and it is settled.

00:06:31.185 --> 00:06:31.665
And

00:06:31.985 --> 00:06:34.520
the problem is, is as

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:35.640
the

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.880
water gets higher in the velocity of the water increases,

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.920
the Venturi effect sucks the

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:44.040
sand

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:49.725
gravel mix out of the hole in the bottom of the culvert

00:06:50.205 --> 00:06:54.685
and undermines the sides of the culvert, and you get sinkholes.

00:06:54.845 --> 00:06:58.845
If you look at the picture, can I showed you basically where the two sinkholes

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:00.460
appeared?

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:04.620
So, what the town has done for an emergency fix

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:07.020
is they have dug,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.500
staying within the concrete

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:11.340
head walls,

00:07:12.235 --> 00:07:13.915
upstream and downstream,

00:07:14.235 --> 00:07:16.315
they've dug out the film material.

00:07:17.595 --> 00:07:23.835
And then first put down about six inch riprap, so basically heavy ballast stone,

00:07:24.235 --> 00:07:25.195
filter fabric,

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:32.310
and then filled it back up with a very low strength concrete mixture called flowable fill.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:34.870
So, there's nothing there to

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:36.870
disintegrate and wash out.

00:07:39.755 --> 00:07:42.395
I realize we're doing this a little backwards,

00:07:42.475 --> 00:07:46.715
although we submitted the wetlands application before we started,

00:07:46.875 --> 00:07:51.435
you know, there was almost a month delay before the Wetlands Commission met.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:55.650
And so, part of this work has been completed.

00:07:56.450 --> 00:08:02.610
Public Works would still like to do the downstream side now, which the Board of Select has requested

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:04.690
before we

00:08:06.255 --> 00:08:07.615
reopen Bunker Hill.

00:08:08.975 --> 00:08:09.615
So,

00:08:09.935 --> 00:08:20.570
we monitored very carefully to make sure that while construction was going, we weren't getting a lot of sediment in the river, and we were not. At the time we did it, the

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:22.650
water flow was very low,

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:25.690
and the stream continued to run clear.

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:27.690
We're not anticipating,

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:30.730
you know,

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:31.450
any

00:08:32.385 --> 00:08:35.505
actual work in the river

00:08:36.065 --> 00:08:39.185
without the consent of the Wetlands Commission.

00:08:39.985 --> 00:08:42.785
The other area that's of concern

00:08:43.105 --> 00:08:45.505
is if you see on that picture I'm showing,

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.660
says area of localized scour.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:09:02.205
There's actually a scour hole on the head wall that extends below the concrete head wall, which means the head wall along the front and part of there is pretty well undermined and kind of structurally

00:09:02.925 --> 00:09:04.765
not in the best of shape.

00:09:06.045 --> 00:09:07.645
We would consider

00:09:07.645 --> 00:09:09.965
using heavy balanced rock

00:09:10.445 --> 00:09:14.125
in there to fill that scour hole to prevent

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:17.620
further undermining of the headwall in case we got another,

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:19.780
you know, high flow,

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:22.100
but that appears to be,

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:24.900
that would require permission

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:26.900
of the Welles Commission.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:27.780
So,

00:09:28.265 --> 00:09:30.985
in a nutshell, that's what we're talking about doing.

00:09:31.225 --> 00:09:34.905
